  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64193,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 286,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cervix</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:42:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64194,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 286,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cervical atrophy is asymptomatic or associated with symptoms related to vaginal atrophy. Colposcopically, glandular ectopy is absent because the transformation zone and squamocolumnar junction have repositioned proximally into the endocervical canal. The thin, vulnerable squamous mucosa appears pale with subepithelial petechial haemorrhages {20042564}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:42:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64195,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 286,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Atrophy is present in prepuberty, after menopause, during the postpartum period, and in association with antiestrogenic therapy (including, occasionally, oral contraception).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:42:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64196,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 286,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Low estrogen levels are responsible for atrophy.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:42:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64197,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 286,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In the absence of estrogens, keratinocytes of the squamous epithelium do not mature beyond parabasal cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:42:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64198,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 286,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:42:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64199,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 286,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The epithelium is only a few cell layers thick and is composed of parabasal cells with scant pale grey/blue cytoplasm and slightly enlarged uniform nuclei with minor variations in size and orientation {14560533}. Maturation into superficial cells with glycogen deposition does not occur. Mitoses are typically absent. Severely atrophic epithelium can be densely infiltrated by neutrophils, occasionally accompanied by intraepithelial oedema (spongiosis) with consequent desquamation of monolayer sheets. Degenerative and regenerative changes can be considerable with occasional nuclear hyperchromasia and/or nuclear contour irregularities. Atrophic squamous keratinocytes may show perinuclear haloes (pseudokoilocytosis). Atrophy can mimic thin or atrophic squamous intraepithelial lesions. Atrophic squamous epithelium is p16-negative and shows a low Ki-67 proliferation index {10727980; 10202668; 15870531}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:42:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64200,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 286,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Atrophy occurs as flat monolayer sheets or a predominance of single parabasal cells. The nuclei are generally enlarged and may be dark, but they are round to oval and uniform, with smooth nuclear membranes and even chromatin. A background of granular debris, inflammation, amorphous basophilic material, and degenerating parakeratotic cells in the setting of atrophic vaginitis can mimic tumour diathesis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:42:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64201,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 286,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:42:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64202,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 286,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> thin stratified epithelium without maturation and mitoses.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> in cases with atypia, negative or patchy p16 staining and a low Ki-67 proliferation index.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:42:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64203,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 286,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:42:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64204,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 286,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Atrophy can be reversed or improved by local or systemic hormone replacement. Repeat biopsy/smear after estrogenization should reveal maturation and substantially decreased atypia.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:42:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]